Intuitively, the convergence rate should depend on the distance between $x^k$ and $z^k$. When these points are close (e.g., $x^k = z^k$), the stochastic gradient is computed near the update point, typically yielding descent on average. In contrast, if they are far apart, the gradient at $z^k$ may poorly approximate the local behavior of $f$ at $x^k$, making the update direction irrelevant. Thus, it is crucial to define a suitable distance metric that is both easy to evaluate for any point pair and directly related to the convergence speed of the \algname{SGD} method. We propose the following:
\begin{definition}
  \label{def:dist}
