\textbf{2.~Number of model updates.} At the same time, comparing \eqref{eq:async_sgd} and \eqref{eq:BxYrFsyLoVzhayw} reveals that \algname{Rennala SGD} computes $B$ stochastic gradients at the same point, while \algname{Ringmaster ASGD} both computes and “explores” more by immediately updating the model as in \eqref{eq:async_sgd}. This feature makes \algname{Ringmaster ASGD} more practically appealing. In fact, this intuition can be formalized using a simple two-dimensional strongly convex quadratic function $f : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x,y) = \mu x^2 / 2 + L y^2 / 2$ for all $x,y \in \mathbb{R}.$ For this function, we prove that \algname{Rennala SGD} requires $\widetilde{\Theta}\left({\color{red}\nicefrac{\sigma^2}{\varepsilon n}} \times h \times \nicefrac{L}{\mu}\right)$
seconds to achieve an $\varepsilon$--solution under the $h_i$-fixed computation model with $h_i = h$ for all $i \in [n]$, while \algname{Ringmaster ASGD} needs $\widetilde{\Theta}\left(h \times \nicefrac{L}{\mu}\right),$ which is $\nicefrac{\sigma^2}{\varepsilon n}$ seconds less (see formal result in Section~\ref{sec:quadratic}). \textbf{This is the first result showing that \algname{Ringmaster ASGD}/\algname{Asynchronous SGD} can be \emph{strongly} better than \algname{Rennala SGD}.}
